          <caption>
            <p>Subconjunctival Hemorrhage. This image displays a well-circumscribed, bright red patch beneath the conjunctiva of the eye, sharply demarcated from the surrounding sclera. The hemorrhage is nontender, does not cross the limbus, and is limited anteriorly. No signs of swelling, discharge, or involvement of the cornea are visible. The adjacent skin and periorbital tissues appear normal. The&#x000a0;patient is on anticoagulant medication. Contributed by Stephan Moll, MD</p>
